# Half-Life's crossbow, the HD rig. Measured by vrcardgen off
# valve_hd/models/v_crossbow.mdl, cut and carded by hand.
#
# ONE CARD, THREE GAME DIRECTORIES - BUT NOT BECAUSE THE FILES ARE THE SAME.
# valve_hd, gearbox_hd and bshift_hd ship three DIFFERENT crossbow files:
# 229476, 257492 and 281688 bytes, and 1011, 1017 and 989 vertices. What they
# share is the rig and the weapon - 73 bones with the same names in the same
# order, 12 sequences, hash 0xb6f36d2f, and every vertex of the crossbow
# itself in the same place. The three differ only in the arm and hand mesh,
# which is not the weapon. Checked: every geometric number in this card -
# the two grab points, both radii, all four boxes quoted below - comes out
# identical on all three files.
#
# The SD rigs are genuinely different models and want their own cards: valve
# is 56 bones / 0x60e2e198, gearbox 70 / 0x4000585d, bshift 55 / 0x6bb29bce.
# They do share this one's travels (Slide 9.96, Clip Master 8.36), so the
# mechanism below should port; the geometry must be re-measured.
#
# Part J: "string `Slide` (10.0); bolt rack `Clip Master` (8.4). Draw the
# string back to its catch, lay a bolt in the groove, fire." Part D puts it
# under single shot, and the `single_shot` preset names this weapon in so
# many words: "The crossbow gives it a bone and a sear, and then drawing the
# string IS the reload."
#
# THE MODEL HANDS THE WHOLE MECHANISM OVER, which is rare. Measured:
#
#   `Slide` is the string and its nut, 48 vertices, travelling 9.96 units
#   along the stock. Its two ends are the two states of the weapon: at
#   local y -4.76 the string is home (released), at +5.10 it is drawn, and
#   the model's own bind pose is the drawn end, 99% of travel. `fire1`
#   runs it from drawn to home in six frames and then spends forty-two
#   drawing it back again, which is this animation's own opinion of how
#   long a re-draw takes - about a second and a half.
#
#   THE LIMBS ARE NOT JOINTS, THEY ARE THE STRING'S CONSEQUENCE. `LA1..LA4`
#   and `RA1..RA4` flex by 0.18 and 0.20 radians, and they do it in exact
#   lockstep with the string in every sequence either of them moves in -
#   string at 0.00 of travel gives LA1 0.000 rad, 0.33 gives 0.031, 0.55
#   gives 0.059, 0.72 gives 0.086, 0.92 gives 0.142, 0.99 gives 0.183, with
#   no frame anywhere that moves one without the other. A renderer lerping
#   the bracketed pose by the string's joint value therefore bends the bow
#   for free. Declaring the limbs as joints would hand the player two
#   hinges that mean nothing and let them flex a bow whose string has not
#   moved.
#
#   `Clip Master` is the bolt rack: 8.35 units down the z axis plus a
#   0.46-radian pitch and a 0.41-radian roll, all one gesture, out and back
#   again inside `reload` frames 12-48. It owns no mesh itself; its
#   children do - `Bone20`, the 13-vertex plate that hangs under the stock,
#   and four reserve bolt tails on `Ammomid`, `Ammomid01..03`.
#
# A COMPOUND MOTION IS STILL ONE JOINT. Unlike the M40A1's bolt, the rack's
# drop and its tilt are not two independent axes a hand could work
# separately - they are one pull, and the animation moves them together on
# one curve. One value drives the lerp and the whole gesture comes with it,
# so the rack needs no carving.

weapon v_crossbow
match  bones 73  seqs 12  hash 0xb6f36d2f

# NO CAPACITY AND NO RPM, BOTH DELIBERATE.
#
# `single_shot` reads neither: there is no magazine and no tube, the groove
# holds the one bolt, and VRFeed_Clip reports 1 or 0 for a weapon flagged
# SINGLE. A `capacity 5` here would look like Half-Life's clip size and
# would be read by nothing.
#
# The rate is left at the parser's default - 60 ms, a thousand a minute -
# because on this weapon the rate is NOT the limiter and must not pretend
# to be. Nothing fires until the string is back on its sear, and the
# animation says that is about a second and a half of hand work. Any rpm
# low enough to bite would be a second, invisible timer refusing a shot
# from a player who has drawn the string and laid a bolt in.
type   single_shot

# AND IT WILL NOT FIRE UNTIL IT IS COCKED. The preset sets SINGLE and
# nothing else, because the other single shot in the game - the RPG - has no
# string to draw. This is the flag that makes drawing the string the reload.
feed   needs_cock

# JOINT 0: THE STRING, and joint 0 is where `single_shot` puts the action
# (feed.action_joint = 0), so it cannot go anywhere else.
#
# `rest 0` is the released end, and it has to be: the limbs are the spring,
# and a bow pulls its string FORWARD. That is also what makes firing work -
# the sear lets go and the string runs home under the same spring a hand was
# fighting.
#
# The cock point and the sear are the preset's own 90% and 95% of travel,
# left alone because there is nothing on this model to measure them against:
# the sear is inside the stock and has no bone. At 9.96 units of travel they
# fall at 8.96 and 9.46, which is a string drawn all the way back to the nut,
# and the bind pose sits at 99% - just past the catch, where a cocked
# crossbow belongs.
#
# The grab point is the cocking handle, not the middle of the part. 28 of
# the string bone's 48 vertices are a tab out at x 3.54..4.09 dropping to
# z -3.10 - a handle on the side of the stock - and their centroid and
# enclosing radius are the numbers below. The other 20 are the nut and the
# string itself, on the centreline behind the bolt's nock, which is not
# something a hand closes on.
joint 0  bone "Slide"  slide  travel 9.96  rest 0
    grab  point 3.77 -2.66 -0.73  radius 2.40

# JOINT 1: THE BOLT RACK, AND IT FEEDS NOTHING. Under `single` the bolt
# comes from the pouch and goes in the groove by hand, so the rack is not
# the magazine and gets no `mag_clear` and no `mag_latch` - on a weapon with
# mag_joint unset both would be points nothing listens to, which is the one
# thing a card must never contain.
#
# It is here because it is a real part with real geometry and a measured
# travel, and a player should be able to swing it open and look at the four
# bolts in it. It carries no catch for the same reason the M40A1's bolt
# handle carries none: there is no latch bone on this model to measure, and
# a catch with no modelled release is a part that locks shut forever.
#
# Grabbed on the bolts rather than on the plate. The four reserve tails are
# 112 vertices clustered under the stock; that centroid and enclosing radius
# are the numbers below, and the sphere clears the string's handle by 1.6
# units so the two parts cannot be grabbed by mistake for one another.
joint 1  bone "Clip Master"  slide  travel 8.35  rest 0
    grab  point 0.00 -6.49 -5.68  radius 3.30

# NO `chamber at`, BECAUSE THE MODEL ALREADY DRAWS THE BOLT. `chamber_at`
# exists for a weapon whose chambered round has no geometry; this one has
# `Bone18`, 41 vertices of bolt from y -14.67 to -6.39, parented through
# `Bolt` to the string so it rides the draw and goes with the shot.
# Declaring a chamber position would draw a second bolt beside the first.
#
# WHAT THE MODEL DOES INSTEAD OF HIDING THINGS: when there is no bolt
# loaded, `Bolt` is translated 45.17 units behind the weapon - at `reload`
# frame 0 its mesh sits at y +20.59..+28.87, twenty units past the back of
# a body that ends at +6.86 - and a spent reserve bolt is parked 50.02 units
# away the same way. Those two travels are the largest numbers in the model
# and neither is a joint; they are off-screen, which is this engine's way of
# saying invisible. Anything that chooses to show or hide the bolt itself
# wants to know that.
#
# NO `eject`, because SINGLE means nothing is left behind: "A crossbow bolt
# and a rocket leave the weapon entirely - there is no case to eject."
#
# NO CONTROL. The sear is released by the trigger, in VRFeed_Fire, which is
# the whole of how a crossbow works. A `control sear` would be mapped to
# stick-pulled-back by Part G and would give the player a second, silent way
# to dump the string off its catch.
#
# NOT JOINTS, and worth saying so: `Ammo Rotator` turns 0.131 radians -
# roughly the 0.105 that separates two of the four `Ammobone` spokes - which
# is the rack indexing itself one place per shot. It is decoration on a feed
# path that holds one bolt. `Bone18` is the bolt. `Bone20` and the four
# `Ammomid` tails are the rack's own mesh and move with it.
#
# LEFT AT DEFAULTS, every one of them for want of something to measure: the
# string's spring, damping and recoil (a bow's limbs are stiff, and nothing
# in a .mdl says how stiff); the strike, so this inherits Part J's
# pistol-whip default; and the muzzle, because THIS MODEL HAS NO
# ATTACHMENTS AT ALL - numattachments is 0, so there is no muzzle_pos to
# read and `_frame` falls back to -y. The forward direction used throughout
# the measurements above is -y, established from the geometry instead: the
# bow limbs root at y -19.50 and the body's vertices run from y -20.64 to
# +6.86, so the business end is -y.
